I'd dispute this.

This used to be a major complaint with Nethack, that winning with a
Wizard was more or less the same as winning with a Barbarian.  It's
been reduced somewhat with the weapon skill limitations and
spellcasting abilities, but not enough.  I base this on my recent
Tourist ascension in which I found a two-handed sword very early, and
suddenly my game was about as easy as it was with my Valkyrie
ascension -- even easier in some ways, because I had an absurdly high
constitution, and hit points were plentiful in the early game.

Once I got that sword, the game quickly became very valkyrie-like. 
When I got GDSM from a magic lamp in Minetown things got even easier,
and finding Mjollnir in the Castle completed the picture.

Though not having that half physical damage from the Orb of Fate made
things tricky on the Astral plane, the only other thing that really
separated the two games was that I couldn't reach Skilled or Expert in
those two weapons.

In the long run, many classes still end up more or less the same,
unless you're playing a Wizard (who I think has been
well-differentiated).  The early game is where the classes differ the
most, while towards the end the need to fill the shopping list of
intrinsics gives most classes the same, or similar, abilities.  The
Quest artifact remedies this somewhat.

